A displacement a few bytes BELOW an entity's base is a BIASED ARRAY REFERENCE.

cl folds the constant part of `arr[i - k]` into the addressing mode, so the
operand carries `arr - k` and the effective address is only ever `arr + i`.
That biased constant points at no entity at all - it is short of the array's
base - so `is_addr` rejects it and BOTH sides print the raw number. The two
numbers then differ purely because the two images are based differently, and
an otherwise byte-identical function can never reach 100%.

Note this is the exact opposite failure to patches 04/07/09/10/11, which
SUPPRESS naming so that both sides print the same raw number. That works
because a struct member offset really is the same number in both images. A
biased array base is not: it is an address, and it has to be NAMED on both
sides to compare equal.

The check runs BEFORE the is_addr gate, because the biased constant can also
land inside whatever entity happens to precede the array - which resolves, but
to the wrong thing, and (thanks to /base) only on the original side.

Regression check: BackGround::videoName @0x41add0, which tests
`buf[strlen(buf) - 3]`, `- 4` and `- 5` and stores through each of them - six
operands, 91.04% without this patch with every byte already correct.

